# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
# Qwen3.8-Flash-Next, a 125B MoE (512 experts, 10 active, 48 layers): every routed expert in system
# RAM, file-backed; attention, the shared expert and the KV cache on the card. Experimental, a side
# experiment: measured for speed on one machine (7800X3D, 64 GB with 50 GB given to WSL2), not in an
# agent session. 9-10 tok/s, prompts at ~36. Needs ~48 GB of RAM to run at that speed.
# See docs/qwen.md#qwen38-flash-125b-experimental.
# Sourced by config.env after the profile, so a profile value wins over the defaults here.

# Unsloth's prebuilt llama.cpp, the one Unsloth Studio installs, not a tree this repo builds. Its
# sparse attention runs a banded flash-attention kernel (FLASH_ATTN_EXT_BANDED); mainline 8212c78
# and master of 2026-09-29 run it dense over a full mask, which needs VRAM at runtime that the
# buffers above leave no room for: it died at ~2.5k tokens of prefill twice, at the same 7 386 MiB
# the prebuilt runs deep with. Its source is a release tarball, not a fetchable commit, so the pin
# is the build number, which build checks. T-038 is making Unsloth's tree the one Qwen builds on.
: "${LLAMA_PREBUILT:=unsloth}"
: "${LLAMA_BUILD:=11160}"
: "${LLAMA_DIR:=$HOME/.unsloth/llama.cpp}"
# Without the CUDA 13 runtime from the Studio venv on the library path, the prebuilt runs on the
# CPU alone and says nothing about it.
: "${LLAMA_LIB_PATH:=$HOME/.unsloth/studio/unsloth_studio/lib/python3.13/site-packages/nvidia/cu13/lib}"
PATCH_DIR=""

# UD-IQ4_XS, 87.2 GiB in three parts: routed experts 55.4, an n-gram embedding table 26.8 that is
# read lazily and does not limit speed, the rest ~4.4 on the card. One checksum per part.
: "${MODEL_REPO:=unsloth/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-GGUF}"
: "${MODEL_REV:=38bb39ee97821de2c9009abb7e93950eec396e66}"
: "${MODEL_FILE:=UD-IQ4_XS/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-UD-IQ4_XS-00001-of-00003.gguf}"
: "${MODEL_SHA256:=5ce89370720f8bf90890f439361282104c1aa1482d4013bb9a50923e758e71a4 577a38a2392b40ca2193cea502e1d92f60b8cd370675d308e0ec21885d9daaa7 d4634e6d84f0ebb0940be15c90d3790bf6464e3dea3a1cddc567dc0e83ad8833}"
: "${MODEL_ALIAS:=qwen3.8-flash}"
# The experts are read through the page cache: with 50 GB for WSL2 it holds ~48 GB of the file and
# decodes at ~10 tok/s; at 30 GB the SSD is in the loop and it drops to ~6. Preflight asks for this
# plus ~2 GB in total. Measured on CUDA only.
MODEL_DISK_MB=89400
MODEL_RAM_MB=46000
MODEL_BACKENDS="cuda"

: "${KV_K:=q8_0}"
: "${KV_V:=q8_0}"
# Unlike Qwen3.6, this template reads reasoning_effort (low, medium, xhigh, its default) the way
# Bonsai's does. medium as for Bonsai: at ~10 tok/s every thinking token costs a tenth of a second.
: "${EFFORT:=medium}"
# No MTP: the shared-Q8_0 head gives +0-5 % once the experts are cached, costs ~1.1 GB of VRAM,
# and ran the card out of memory at 64k.
: "${SPEC_TYPE:=}"

# --no-repack keeps the experts file-backed, so the page cache holds them instead of anonymous
#   memory that WSL2 swaps out.
# --no-op-offload runs prompt processing on the CPU instead of copying 55 GB of experts over PCIe
#   per ubatch: less VRAM, and faster on the short prompts of a tool loop.
# -cram 0 and -ctxcp 4: llama.cpp keeps 8 GB of prompt cache and 32 checkpoints in RAM by default,
#   which evicts experts from the page cache.
: "${SERVER_ARGS:=--no-repack --no-op-offload -cram 0 -ctxcp 4}"
